    <div style="background:#10233d;padding:20px;border-radius:14px;margin-bottom:20px;border:1px solid #33415c;border-left:4px solid #22d3ee;">
    <h3 style="color:#ffcdd2;margin-top:0;">🔭 Scientific Background</h3>
    <p>In a <strong>two-stroke engine</strong>, there are no valves in the simplest designs; instead the piston uncovers <strong>ports</strong> in the cylinder wall. As the piston rises, it compresses the mixture above it and draws fresh mixture into the crankcase below. As it falls after combustion, it compresses the mixture in the crankcase, then uncovers the exhaust port and the transfer port, so fresh mixture pushes out the burnt gases (<strong>scavenging</strong>). Two-stroke engines are light and simple but waste some fuel and produce more pollution, because some fresh mixture escapes with the exhaust and oil is burned with the fuel.</p>
    </div>
